2027 Signing Projection Ideas
Oct 4, 2026 · @Geoffel Spilker
Ideas for turning the 2026 free agent rankings into a 2027 signing plan, grouped by what each one fixes. Start with the Statcast luck check.
Make the scores more trustworthy
Every score today comes from one season of stats, so these ideas test how much of it is real.
• Statcast luck check: compare actual results to expected results (xwOBA, barrel rate, hard-hit rate) to flag likely regression and buy-low candidates. The data is already in data/.
• 2027 player projections: blend the last 3 seasons, weight recent ones more, regress small samples toward average and apply an age adjustment. Needs the 2024 and 2025 league leaderboards.
• Park adjustment: correct for home parks, since T-Mobile Park plays tough on hitters.
• Durability: discount players who produce but miss a lot of games; injury_cost_track.py may be reusable.
Price signings properly
Cost is currently last year's salary, which leaves out several real costs.
• Dollars per WAR: WAR is already in the free agent data but unused in scoring. This shows whether a price is fair against typical market rates.
• Qualifying offer and pick cost: the "QOI" tag in the Spotrac data may mean signing the player costs a draft pick. Needs confirming.
• Contract length and aging: price each deal over its whole length, not just year one.
Turn it into decisions
These turn the ranked lists into a single plan.
• Budget slate picker: give it a payroll number and it picks the best combination, one player per position, handling overlaps like Suzuki at RF or DH.
• 2027 season projection: simulate Seattle's 2027 with and without the signings, using montecarlo.py and the 2027 schedule, to get projected wins and division and playoff odds. Needs a strength estimate for all 30 teams.
• Prospects on the ranked list: add near-ready AA and AAA players, with minor league stats converted to a rough MLB equivalent and adjusted for level and park, so "sign someone or play the kid" is one comparison.
• Prospect-block check: flag signings that would sit in front of a ready prospect like Montes or Emerson.
• Lineup balance by handedness: score how each signing changes the lineup against lefties and righties. Needs bats-handed data added.
• Who else is bidding: run the diagnosis for all 30 teams to see who competes for each target.
Suggested build order
The first three make the scores more reliable; the last two turn them into a decision.
1. Statcast luck check (small, data already in hand)
2. 2027 player projections
3. Dollars per WAR
4. Budget slate picker
5. 2027 season simulation with and without the signings
Everything else can be added whenever it matters.
